    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/40375a9794d9e399327cfcf128e017f05d20319fd86d41a8a9f49e01f58ccae0.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=4$  (shown above) at $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$",
    "worked_solution": "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =7\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
